</a>

        <h2 id="step-by-step">How to Write a Master's Thesis Step by Step</h2>

        <p>To make this extensive project manageable, follow this 
        practical roadmap:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Establish a timeline working 
          backward from your submission deadline.</li>
          <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Conduct a systematic 
          literature search using Google Scholar, Scopus, or your 
          university library.</li>
          <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write the methodology section 
          while your research design is fresh.</li>
          <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> Draft your body chapters — 
          literature review, results, and discussion.</li>
          <li><strong>Step 5:</strong> Write the introduction and 
          conclusion last for greater accuracy.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="checklist">Master's Thesis Checklist You Can Copy</h2>

        <p>Feel free to copy this checklist into your own planning 
        document or cite it directly if you're writing about the 
        thesis process yourself.</p>

        <div class="linkable-checklist">
          <ul>
            <li>☐ Research question is specific, feasible, and analytically complex</li>
            <li>☐ Proposal approved by supervisor before full drafting begins</li>
            <li>☐ Methodology section drafted while research is still fresh</li>
            <li>☐ Literature review identifies a clear gap, not just a summary</li>
            <li>☐ Every chapter ties back to the original research question</li>
            <li>☐ Reference list matches your university's required citation style</li>
            <li>☐ Full draft reviewed for structure before line-editing begins</li>
            <li>☐ Defense presentation rehearsed in under 15 minutes</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

